---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import VicRegionMap from "../../components/VicRegionMap.astro";
import { loadElection } from "../../../../scripts/lib/data.mjs";

const data = loadElection("vic2026");
const regions = [...data.regions].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const districts = [...data.districts].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const totalDistricts = districts.length;

const byRegion = regions.map((r) => ({
  region: r,
  districts: districts.filter((d) => d.region === r.slug),
}));
---

<Base
  title="Victorian electoral districts"
  description="Map and short history of Victoria’s 88 Legislative Assembly districts — how they sit in 8 Council regions, the 2021 redivision, and a few seats with notable past figures. Candidate lists live on the Assembly page."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <header class="page-head">
      <div class="page-head-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Victoria · District atlas</p>
        <h1>Electoral districts</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Victoria’s lower house is divided into
          <strong>{totalDistricts} single-member districts</strong>. Each elects one member of
          the Legislative Assembly by preferential voting. The same geography is nested inside
          <strong>8 Legislative Council regions</strong> (<span class="mono">8 × 11 = 88</span>).
        </p>
        <p class="muted house-switch">
          Candidate lists &amp; sitting members:
          <a href="/assembly">Legislative Assembly</a>
          {" · "}
          <a href="/regions">Legislative Council</a>
        </p>
        <p class="muted atlas-note">
          This page is a map-first atlas with short historical notes — not linked from the main
          menu. For 2026 candidacies, use the Assembly index.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="page-head-map">
        <VicRegionMap mode="assembly" compact />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="prose-block" aria-labelledby="how-h">
      <h2 id="how-h">How the map works</h2>
      <p>
        Click a region on the schematic map to jump to that region’s districts on the
        <a href="/assembly">Assembly</a> page (or open the matching
        <a href="/regions">Council</a> region for the five upper-house seats). Boundaries are
        from the <strong>2021 redivision</strong> by the Electoral Boundaries Commission; the
        map itself is a navigation diagram, not survey geometry.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the mid-1980s the Assembly has had 88 seats. Region names and the exact set of
        11 districts per region change when boundaries are redrawn, but the
        <span class="mono">8 × 11</span> pattern for the lower house and
        <span class="mono">8 × 5 = 40</span> for the Council has been stable since the Council
        moved to multi-member regions in 2006.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-block" aria-labelledby="hist-h">
      <h2 id="hist-h">Past figures and notable seats</h2>
      <p class="muted section-lead">
        A few districts and former divisions are remembered for the people who held them or the
        roles those members later took. This is not a complete parliamentary history — only
        pointers while the atlas is young. District names below match current or recent usage
        where possible.
      </p>
      <ul class="figures" role="list">
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/districts/mulgrave">Mulgrave</a></strong>
          — long associated with Daniel Andrews (Premier 2014–2023), who held the seat from its
          recreation in 2002 until his resignation from Parliament.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/districts/broadmeadows">Broadmeadows</a></strong>
          — a long-standing Labor seat in Melbourne’s north; John Brumby held Broadmeadows
          while Treasurer and as Premier (2007–2010).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/districts/hawthorn">Hawthorn</a></strong>
          and
          <strong><a href="/districts/kew">Kew</a></strong>
          — inner-eastern seats that have produced Liberal leadership figures over the decades;
          Kew was held by Ted Baillieu before and during his premiership (2010–2013).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/districts/williamstown">Williamstown</a></strong>
          — waterfront Labor seat historically linked to senior party figures; Joan Kirner
          represented Williamstown before and while Premier (1990–1992).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/districts/richmond">Richmond</a></strong>
          and
          <strong><a href="/districts/melbourne">Melbourne</a></strong>
          — inner-city seats that have been early testing grounds for minor-party and
          independent challenges alongside major-party contests.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/districts/mildura">Mildura</a></strong>
          ,
          <strong><a href="/districts/shepparton">Shepparton</a></strong>
          and other regional seats — often the focus of Nationals–Liberal and independent
          contests, reflecting different country and regional coalitions over time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Abolished or renamed divisions</strong>
          — redivisions retire names (for example older suburban and rural labels) and mint
          new ones (such as Eureka, Greenvale, Kalkallo after the 2021 review). Sitting members
          sometimes follow their communities into a renamed seat rather than “lose” in a simple
          sense.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted fine-print">
        Biographical notes are for orientation only. For authoritative past results and
        official maps, use the Victorian Electoral Commission and the Parliament of Victoria
        records. This site’s district pages track <em>sourced 2026 candidacies</em>, not full
        historical member rolls.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="all-h">
      <h2 id="all-h">All {totalDistricts} districts</h2>
      <p class="muted section-lead">
        A–Z index. Each link opens the district page (sitting member and any recorded 2026
        candidates). Grouped view by region:
        <a href="/assembly">Assembly index</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="az">
        {
          districts.map((d) => (
            <a href={`/districts/${d.slug}`} class="az-item">
              {d.name}
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h3 class="subh">By Council region</h3>
      <ul class="region-lists" role="list">
        {
          byRegion.map(({ region, districts: ds }) => (
            <li class="card region-card">
              <h4>
                <a href={`/regions/${region.slug}`}>{region.name}</a>
                <span class="muted count">{ds.length}</span>
              </h4>
              <ul class="mini" role="list">
                {ds.map((d) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href={`/districts/${d.slug}`}>{d.name}</a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
              <p class="muted card-foot">
                <a href={`/assembly#region-${region.slug}`}>Assembly table →</a>
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="foot muted">
      Schematic map only. Official boundaries: Electoral Boundaries Commission / VEC.
      {" "}
      Main 2026 candidate index: <a href="/assembly">Legislative Assembly</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .mono {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .page-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 880px) {
    .page-head {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(15rem, 20rem);
    }
  }
  .page-head-copy .lede {
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
  }
  .house-switch {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .atlas-note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    max-width: 36rem;
  }
  .prose-block {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .prose-block h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.65rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }
  .prose-block p {
    margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .section-lead {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .figures {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .figures li strong a {
    font-weight: 650;
  }
  .fine-print {
    font-size: 0.86rem;
  }
  .az {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.35rem 0.45rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
  }
  .az-item {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 560;
    padding: 0.28rem 0.55rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .az-item:hover {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }
  .subh {
    margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .region-lists {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .region-card h4 {
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .region-card h4 a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .region-card h4 a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .count {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .mini {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.28rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }
  .card-foot {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 0.55rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
  }
  .foot {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
